275 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (The painting system runs on the Raspberry Pi 5, and the Mac mini M4 sends it requests through HTTP. There are five peristaltic pumps, one for each base color \(blue, yellow, white, black, and red\). Each pump is connected to a driver and controlled through two pins, IN1 and IN2, using the lgpio library on the Pi 5. During testing, we noticed that each pump dispenses a different amount of paint per second, so we measured the flow rate of each one and stored it in the code in milliliters per second. To dispense a specific amount, the program divides the required milliliters by the pump's flow rate, which determines how many seconds it needs to remain on. To prepare an artwork, the service receives the colors for each stage and calculates the recipe for each one using the primary colors, comparing the result with the Pantone color selected for that shade. The stepper motor that moves the paint container over the palette is controlled by a Raspberry Pi 4 with an A4988 driver. The Pi 5 sends it commands through a serial cable \(UART at 9600 baud, with a common ground\). The motor always runs at the same speed, 3,200 pulses per second. Two limit switches, MIN and MAX, stop the carriage as soon as they are triggered. The zero position is set when the carriage reaches the MIN limit switch. From that point, the program knows how many steps away each of the five color wells is, allowing it to move directly to any of them.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
276 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (Brush Cleaning System)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
277 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 182 0 R
/E (An infrared barrier detects when the paintbrush reaches the cleaning area. It consists of an infrared transmitter and a 38 kHz receiver. Since the receiver becomes inactive when exposed to continuous light, the transmitter emits 80 ms bursts every 300 ms, generated using hardware PWM. This timing also serves as the heartbeat for the service, which runs continuously on the Pi 5. When several consecutive bursts confirm that something is interrupting the beam, preventing the system from reacting to a single reading, the submersible water pump turns on for 5 seconds. During this time, all other inputs are ignored. Once this period ends, the system checks again: if the paintbrush is still there, it runs the pump for another 5 seconds; if not, it resumes monitoring.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 182 0 R
/Pg 21 0 R
/A [624 0 R 0]
/K [4]
>>
endobj
279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
