/P 183 0 R
/T (Central Server and Mac Mini M4 Control Panel:)
/E (Central Server and Mac Mini M4 Control Panel:)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E (The Mac mini M4 is the center of PIXARTEK: all the software runs on it, and it sends commands to the Raspberry Pis. Its operation can be divided into three parts: the application used by the student, the background services, and the way everything communicates. The application is programmed in SwiftUI and works as the control panel. In it, the student's name is entered, an artwork is selected from the catalog, progress through each stage is monitored using the camera and paintbrush, and the user moves from one stage to the next. It opens in full-screen kiosk mode and uses two displays, one for the controls and the other for the projector. When a session begins, the app starts the vision service, turns on the cameras, and informs Hermes of the selected artwork and stage. When the session ends, it shuts everything down and leaves the projector screen black. Behind the application, Python services run independently, each as a separate process with its own port. These include the vision service \(8765\), the five bots \(brushes 8766, color 8772, trajectory 8773, contour 8774, and scene 8775\), Hermes \(8770\), and Chatterbox voice \(8771\). Ollama also runs the hermes3:8b language model, along with Qdrant, the database where Hermes stores its knowledge. They are managed by launchd, the macOS startup system, which launches them automatically when the Mac turns on and allows one to be restarted without affecting the others. Everything communicates through HTTP using JSON messages. The bots send Hermes what they detect, the app sends Hermes the session status, and the app requests paint quantities and rail movements from the Pi 5. This allows the Mac to decide what to do while the Raspberry Pis execute the commands.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 183 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
