284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (One of the main challenges was defining how real-time feedback should work. Initially, we considered a system similar to traditional projected tutorials, where users simply follow visual instructions. However, this did not solve the main problem: the lack of personalized corrections. We realized that without real-time analysis, users could repeatedly make mistakes without noticing them. This led to a key decision: integrating computer vision capable of interpreting the user's brushstrokes and responding dynamically.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (Another important challenge was achieving accurate alignment between the projection and the canvas. Even small errors could affect the learning experience. This required developing advanced calibration systems to ensure that the visual guide matched the physical surface precisely.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (We also faced the challenge of balancing technology with artistic experience. A system that was too automated could make users completely dependent on the machine, reducing their creativity. For this reason, Pixartek was designed as a support tool, not a replacement, keeping the person as the main creator.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (However, one of our biggest challenges was color mixing. Initially, the robot could not mix colors correctly. For example, when we wanted blue, the robot would dispense a color that looked more like gray. We had to work extensively to help the robot correctly interpret the combinations needed to create each specific color.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (Finally, integrating real-time artificial intelligence represented a significant technical challenge. Processing images, interpreting brushstrokes, and responding instantly requires edge computing optimization. This decision was essential to ensure that the system operates smoothly without relying entirely on the cloud.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 184 0 R
/E (DEVELOPMENT CHALLENGES)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
290 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 184 0 R
/Pg 23 0 R
/A [626 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
291 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (Did you know that the world is experiencing a silent crisis in art education and the preservation of traditional cultural practices? In many countries, opportunities to learn painting, understand the history behind artists, and practice hands-on techniques are decreasing. This loss affects not only individual skills but also cultural identity and the way societies express themselves and connect with one another. Pixartek was created to address this issue. It preserves the history of artistic styles and artists, brings traditional painting back to life, and provides personalized guidance so that everyone can learn and develop at their own pace. In our community, it will mainly benefit students and schools looking for new ways to learn art, especially those who do not have access to personalized art education.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
292 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 185 0 R
/T (The Decline of Art Education and the Loss of Cultural Participation:)
/E (The Decline of Art Education and the Loss of Cultural Participation:)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
293 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
294 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (In England, the figures are particularly concerning. According to the Cultural Learning Alliance Report Card 2025, enrollment in GCSE Arts subjects has decreased by 42% since 2010, while the number of Arts teachers has fallen by 27%. This decline limits opportunities to learn and pass artistic techniques on to new generations.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
295 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (This is not just an educational trend; it is a cultural loss. When fewer people participate in art, fewer stories behind artists are preserved, fewer traditional techniques are taught, and the risk of traditional practices disappearing increases.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 185 0 R
/E (SOCIAL IMPACT AND INNOVATION)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 185 0 R
/Pg 24 0 R
/A [627 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 186 0 R
/T (Loss of Cultural Heritage and Artistic Knowledge:)
/E (Loss of Cultural Heritage and Artistic Knowledge:)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 186 0 R
/E (As participation in the arts decreases, exposure to the history, techniques, and stories of influential artists also declines. This phenomenon is not isolated; it reflects a global trend documented by multiple studies on cultural participation.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 186 0 R
/E (According to Eurostat data and European cultural studies, people with lower educational and economic backgrounds participate significantly less in cultural activities, creating a direct gap in access to artistic heritage. This inequality not only limits learning but also determines who has the opportunity to discover, practice, and preserve art. In many cases, access to museums, art education, and cultural experiences can be up to twice as high among people with higher educational levels.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 186 0 R
/E (Furthermore, international reports on art education indicate that, in several countries, the reduction of school art programs has caused entire generations to grow up with little or no exposure to artistic movements, traditional techniques, and key historical figures. This means that artistic knowledge is no longer actively passed down and instead becomes something distant, theoretical, or even nonexistent for many young people.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
302 0 obj
<<
